# A realistic full-project founding body, modelled on the kafka-dq founding
# interview (issue #20 appendix A): one MADR body carrying rules across all
# 5 configured categories (several rules in the same "### architecture"
# heading, since a single founding ADR may open each category heading only
# once — v0.2 grammar), plus a non-mandatory "## Deferred bets" section
# recording three open bets that stay in the log but never project.
# single-container-packaging is filed under "tooling" (a container-build
# concern) rather than "architecture" — matching the render golden fixture
# (internal/render/testdata/golden/realistic) — so this founding ADR's
# rules span architecture+tooling.
#
# The body's "### <category>" headings are deliberately NOT in config
# order (architecture first, purpose second, ...) so this proves grouping
# follows the configured category order, not body order (mirrors
# rules_multi_category.txtar's approach at ADR-new scope, here at init/
# founding scope).

env CONSTITUTION_TODAY=2026-07-02

exec constitution init --founding-file founding.md --category purpose --category architecture --category testing --category tooling --category process --source-tracking none --target claude
stdout 'created .*ADR-0001-founding-constitution\.md'

# the deferred bets are durable in the log ...
grep 'Deferred bets' constitution/adr/ADR-0001-founding-constitution.md
grep 'Micronaut vs\. Quarkus' constitution/adr/ADR-0001-founding-constitution.md
grep 'container-local' constitution/adr/ADR-0001-founding-constitution.md

# ... but record-only content never projects.
! grep 'Deferred bets' constitution/constitution.md
! grep 'Micronaut' constitution/constitution.md
! grep 'container-local' constitution/constitution.md

cmp constitution/constitution.md want-constitution.md

exec constitution guard
stdout 'clean'

-- founding.md --
## Context and Problem Statement

kafka-dq is being founded: an open-source implementation of Confluent
Schema Registry Data Quality Rules, evaluated against live Kafka topics to
track semantic correctness. Recording the project's standing technical
decisions now — purpose, architecture, testing discipline, tooling
constraints, and process — means downstream work never has to re-litigate
them.

## Considered Options

- Leave these decisions implicit and let them emerge ad hoc as the
  project grows
- Record them explicitly as the founding ADR, so every future decision
  starts from a known, citable baseline

## Decision Outcome

Adopt the founding decisions below: conform to Confluent's Data Quality
Rules semantics, structure the system as a library-first, hexagonal,
two-application architecture, test it in three tiers, keep dependencies
redistribution-safe, and run all non-trivial work through spec-lifecycle
gates.

## Rules

### architecture

#### library-first-engine
The rule-evaluation engine is a standalone library, consumable
independently of the Docker deployment, the Kafka runtime, and any JVM
framework. Kafka, transport, storage, and framework concerns live in
applications and adapters that depend on the library — never the reverse.

#### two-application-topology
The engine application and the UI application are separate standalone
applications. They communicate only via Kafka topics — the engine
consumes the source topic and publishes violations; the UI consumes the
violations topic. No shared in-process module and no direct calls between
them.

#### hexagonal-per-skill
Every application follows hexagonal architecture per the `java-hexagonal`
skill. Dependencies point inward; the domain is pure Java with no
framework imports and no I/O; non-JDK libraries in the domain require an
explicit ArchUnit whitelist entry. Any framework is confined to the
composition root, or to a thin framework module delegating to
framework-free wiring underneath.

### purpose

#### confluent-dq-compatibility
Implement the semantics of Confluent Schema Registry Data Quality Rules.
Use CEL (Common Expression Language) as the rule expression syntax. Do not
introduce a proprietary rule syntax or knowingly diverge from Confluent's
semantics; where behaviour is ambiguous, follow Confluent's
implementation.

### process

#### permissive-license
Release this project under a permissive open-source licence (MIT or
Apache-2.0).

#### spec-lifecycle-gates
Take all non-trivial work through spec-lifecycle. Read the relevant
`openspec/changes/<change>/` artefacts before touching related code, and
run `lifecycle status` to see gate state. Approve gates only via
`lifecycle approve` — never by hand-editing `approval-state.json`.

### testing

#### three-tier-test-strategy
Test in the three tiers the `java-hexagonal` skill defines: per-class
unit tests where branching exists and for every mapper; domain tests with
the real object graph and in-memory fakes for outbound ports; integration
tests with Testcontainers and Awaitility. No DI container and no
framework test context in any tier.

#### portable-integration-tests
Integration tests must pass against a standard Docker daemon, not only
inside a proxied-socket environment. Read container ports at runtime and
never hardcode them. The `testcontainers-java` skill is authoritative for
container setup.

### tooling

#### single-container-packaging
Both applications ship inside a single Docker container so the product
deploys as one unit. The container build is the single release artefact;
there is no per-application image and no separate deployment pipeline to
keep in sync.

#### license-compatible-dependencies
Every dependency must be compatible with permissive redistribution. Do not
introduce copyleft (GPL/AGPL/LGPL) or proprietary dependencies.

## Deferred bets

Three items remain open and are recorded here as durable log content, not
standing rules:

- The JVM framework choice (Micronaut vs. Quarkus) is deferred to
  stage-2 research. `hexagonal-per-skill` binds the framework to the
  composition root regardless of which wins.
- UI persistence stays container-local for now; the datastore choice,
  durability guarantees, and retention behaviour are unsettled.
- Library packaging (in-repo module vs. a separately published artefact)
  is undecided; `library-first-engine` constrains the dependency
  direction, not the packaging mechanism.

-- want-constitution.md --
